        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>OBJECTIVES:</B><BR>
The purpose of this study was to determine the minimum G requirements of
exposure to G forces to prevent the deconditioning effects of microgravity
in a ground-based model.  This consisted of evaluating the effectiveness of
intermittent exposure to passive and active +1 Gz force on   deconditioning
manifested as orthostatic intolerance, changes in oxygen uptake, reduced
plasma volume, increases in urinary volume, sodium and calcium excretion
during a period of 4 days ­6<sup>o</sup> head down bed rest.
<P>
<B>APPROACH:</B><BR>
Nine healthy men of average age (38±4.5) yrs were recruited to participate
in a four day bed rest study with each individual tested across five
treatment conditions: Each group consisted of 4 days bed rest with a daily
treatment of 1) no intervention (OG) 2) 4 hours intermittent standing (S4),
3) 4 hours intermittent walking (W4), 4) 2 hours intermittent standing (S2),
5) 2 hours intermittent walking (W2).  Walking was at 3 mph on a level
treadmill. Recovery time in between each treatment was a minimum of one
month.  Measurements of body weight, tilt tolerance, peak VO2, plasma volume
and urinary calcium were carried out just prior to and on the 4th bed rest
day.  Statistical analysis of the five treatments was done by repeated
measures ANOVA with missing estimates adjusted by least squares except for
measurements of heart rate and blood pressure precluded by the large amount
of data.
<P>
<B>RESULTS:</B><BR>
Of interest is that the responses of the physiologic variable to each
treatment differed as a function of the variable measured.  Orthostatic
intolerance was mostly compensated for by an increase in HR.  Standing for 4
hours was most effective with standing for 2 hours more effective than
walking for 2 or 4 hours.  Peak oxygen consumption was best preserved by
walking either 2 or 4 hours and minimally maintained by standing for 4 or 2
hours. Either standing or walking for 4 hours prevented the decrease in
plasma volume.  Walking for either 2 or 4 hours prevented the loss of 4 days
urinary calcium excretion. Absolute values for all data are printed in NASA
TM 108800.
<P>
Conclusions: No single treatment was most effective across all dependent
variables. Preventive effects of different treatments are specific to each
system. Calcium balance responded favorably to activity whereas orthostatic
tolerance and plasma volume were more dependent on the stimulus provided by
the gravitational field.  In most cases, differences between 2 and 4-hour
exposures were small and it was surprising to find out how little exposure
the human body needs to maintain normal health.
        </div>

